6. C-05 — PREVENT CROSS-USER TRANSACTION EXPOSURE AND RAW CLICK ID LEAKAGE

Confirmed audit problem

The audit found that Firestore Rules permit any authenticated user to read all CREDITED transactions.

This exposes other users' financial transaction data.

The audit also found that campaign conversion transaction and notification identifiers include raw clickId values.

Examples identified in the audit:

- conv_credit_conv_<clickId>
- conv_rev_conv_<clickId>
- referenceId: conv_<clickId>
- notif_conv_credit_conv_<clickId>

This violates the intended privacy separation introduced in Phase 3D and Phase 3E.

Required remediation — Transaction Privacy

A. Restrict ordinary /transactions reads to:

- The authenticated owner of the transaction.
- Properly authorized admins.

B. Remove the broad cross-user CREDITED transaction read permission.

C. Verify that the owner comparison uses the actual authenticated UID.

D. Verify that an ordinary user cannot read another user's transaction by guessing or enumerating transaction IDs.

E. Verify that an ordinary user cannot list all credited transactions.

F. Preserve legitimate owner-scoped transaction history.

G. Do not break admin transaction inspection.

H. Do not break the leaderboard.

Required remediation — Leaderboard Compatibility

The current leaderboard implementation uses cross-user credited transaction scanning.

Removing that permission may break the leaderboard.

Do not retain the insecure cross-user read rule just to preserve leaderboard behavior.

Instead:

1. Inspect the complete existing leaderboard architecture.
2. Identify its exact source of earnings data.
3. Move leaderboard aggregation to an appropriate trusted server-side mechanism.
4. Prefer an existing protected leaderboard document or server-maintained aggregate if one is already available.
5. If a new aggregate is necessary, make it server-authoritative.
6. Prevent ordinary clients from writing leaderboard earnings.
7. Preserve correct net earnings calculations.
8. Continue subtracting campaign reversals.
9. Prevent double counting.
10. Ensure the leaderboard cannot be poisoned by forged transactions.
11. Preserve the existing leaderboard UI and navigation.

Do not create an unnecessary second financial source of truth.

Required remediation — Campaign Identifier Privacy

A. Inspect campaign conversion credit and reversal code.

B. Find every place where raw clickId or conv_<clickId> is stored in:

- Transaction document IDs.
- Transaction referenceId.
- Notification document IDs.
- Notification reference fields.
- Publicly readable documents.
- Android response payloads.

C. Replace unnecessary raw clickId-derived public identifiers with opaque, non-reversible activity references.

D. Use the existing activityId/moderationId privacy architecture where compatible.

E. Generate deterministic opaque references using a cryptographically safe server-side mechanism or a properly scoped hash.

F. Preserve idempotency and duplicate transaction prevention.

G. Ensure that credit and reversal records remain reliably associated with their underlying server-side conversion.

H. Keep raw clickId and externalTransactionId in server-only tracking collections when genuinely required for attribution, verification or reconciliation.

I. Do not expose raw clickId in public ledger or user-facing notification documents.

J. Preserve the user's ability to see their own reward history.

K. Do not delete historical records that already contain raw identifiers.

L. Do not silently rename existing historical transaction documents.

M. If legacy records need privacy remediation, provide a separate migration plan without executing it.

Required tests

Verify:

- User A cannot read User B's transaction.
- User A cannot list all CREDITED transactions.
- User A can read their own transactions.
- Authorized Admin can inspect transactions.
- User cannot write their own fake transaction.
- Leaderboard still works.
- Leaderboard does not depend on insecure cross-user client reads.
- Leaderboard subtracts campaign reversals.
- Campaign reward transaction IDs contain no raw clickId.
- Campaign reversal transaction IDs contain no raw clickId.
- Notification IDs and references contain no raw clickId.
- User-facing campaign activity remains readable.
- Duplicate conversion callbacks remain idempotent.

